Hi [~accountid:610d3190ae72b2006f81ee2e],

The external counsel shares my view that both systems of recording the pickers' working hours (i.e. the google forms system and the app system) carry the risk that Bolt is considered the pickers' employer, as the use of these recording systems can be considered as a control of working hours by Bolt, which is typical of an employer.

However, the external counsel says that the qualification of a contract as an employment contract is more complex and depends on more factors than the (simple) monitoring of working hours. To better analyse the risk involved in the use of these tools, external counsel would need more details on how Bolt operates and interacts with the pickers on a day-to-day basis. 

In my opinion, we do not need a more detailed analysis by the external lawyer, because from my knowledge of BM and the way the Ops team interacts with the pickers, I can already say that the use of these tools is not the only risky action from a reclassification point of view that the BM team carries out. Therefore, the combination of several risky practices implies that the reclassification risk, in my opinion, is rather high.

The external lawyer proposed the same solution that I have already proposed, i.e. the agencies controlling/recording pickers’ working hours, and then charging Bolt for the corresponding fees with supporting documents. IMO, this would be the most convenient approach. 



For a better understanding, please find below the full response from external counsel:

{color:#006644}_We agree that both systems are risky. The fact that_ {color}{color:#006644}*_Bolt itself controls pickers’ working hours_*{color}{color:#006644} _may serve as a significant argument for considering that_ {color}{color:#006644}*_pickers are subordinate employees of Bolt_*{color}{color:#006644}_._{color}

{color:#006644}*_Providing pickers with a tool for this purpose_*{color}{color:#006644} _– whether it is a form in Google Doc or an app –_ {color}{color:#006644}*_increases the risk_*{color}{color:#006644}_, since it favours the idea that, rather than simply checking the hours in order to calculate the fees to be paid, Bolt directly controls the pickers' working time. The control of working hours, mainly through mechanisms provided by the beneficiary of the activity, is typical of an employment relationship._{color}

{color:#006644}_So in view of this, considering that pickers are self-employed individuals working for agencies, the risk is related to the_ {color}{color:#006644}*_misclassification of the relationship_*{color}{color:#006644}_, similar to that existing in ride-hailing and couriers._ {color}{color:#006644}*_Pickers may argue that_*{color}{color:#006644}_, despite working under a services agreement for the agency,_ {color}{color:#006644}*_they are indeed Bolt’s employees_*{color}{color:#006644}_._{color}

{color:#006644}_An alternative would be having the_ {color}{color:#006644}*_agencies controlling pickers’ working hours, and then charging Bolt for the corresponding fees_*{color}{color:#006644} _with supporting documents (including, for instance, a detailed description of hours worked by each picker). Could this serve as a viable solution_{color}

{color:#006644}_However, as stated before, the classification of a contract as an employment contract is more complex and depends on more factors than the (simple) control of working hours (e.g., how the activity is performed, if pickers have their performance assessed by Bolt, if they are free to decide when and how to provide the activity, if they respond to orders, who gives them instructions, etc.), so we would need additional information in order to assess how relevant the risk involved is._{color}

{color:#006644}_Regarding the risk of an_ {color}{color:#006644}*_illegal assignment of employees_*{color}{color:#006644}_, we believe it could exist if pickers were considered employees of the agency (and not self-employed workers) assigned to work under Bolt’s direction. In this case, pickers would have to demonstrate that they had an employment contract with the agency and that the agency assigned them to work under Bolt’s orders and instructions._{color}

{color:#006644} _From our perspective, this would be a more complex route to follow, therefore less likely to happen._{color}

{color:#006644}_In cases such as these, the most common approach would be for the picker to argue he/she has an employment relationship with Bolt, rather than a services agreement with the agency._{color}



Should you have any further questions/concerns, please do let me know.

Many thanks!

