So the issues were related to changes in the networking architecture of the k8s cluster for nodejs services:

Long story short, pods in k8s use AWS VPC CNI, so each nodejs service in the cluster has private IP from AWS subnets. To satisfy the current AWS and EKS infrastructure, we took CIDR: 172.17.0.0/16 (for prelive only). And the case is that docker daemon uses the same CIDR by default 

And the issue here is that when mlflow instance responds to a request from nodejs service, the host sees the route on the IP of nodejs container like a local one. We knew about this limitation, but we were fine with that we weren't aware of all edge cases. So there are two ways to solve this issue:

# Disable docker bridge interface
# Define another CIDR for bridge interface

